## Why This Is Asked
Examines ability to tame CPU scheduling and memory reclaim in multi-tenant Linux deployments under bursty workloads.

## Key Concepts
- cgroup v2 cpu.max and CPU weight for per-tenant QoS
- cpuset and CPU affinity to confine tenants
- memory reclaim behavior and swappiness tuning
- BPF-based observability for per-tenant latency, IRQs, cache misses
- phased testing, safe rollback, and controlled rollouts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Lightweight latency probe template (pseudo)
async function measureLatency(endpoint){
  const t0 = performance.now();
  await fetch(endpoint);
  return performance.now() - t0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify safe rollback criteria during staged tests?
- Which metrics dashboards and alerts would you configure for sustained SLOs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical mastery of Linux networking and production stability for data-heavy services, combining NIC topology, traffic isolation, and programmable packet processing.

## Key Concepts
- NIC multi-queue configuration and mqprio
- tc clsact and eBPF-based QoS/policing
- XDP fast-path for critical traffic
- net_cls tagging and CPU isolation
- End-to-end validation with iperf/netperf and DB latency probes

